"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>Payoff Matrix</h2>\n<p>✅ Matrix constructed</p>\n<p>This analysis examines a repeated Cournot competition between two firms (Firm A and Firm B). In this model, firms compete on the <strong>quantity</strong> of a homogeneous product produced, and the market price is determined by the total supply.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 1: Game Structure Analysis</h3>\n<h4>1. Identify the Game Structure</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Non-cooperative. While firms may attempt to collude, there is no binding legal enforcement to ensure they stick to an agreement.</li>\n<li><strong>Repetition:</strong> Repeated game. This is crucial because it allows for &quot;trigger strategies&quot; (punishing a defector in future rounds), which can sustain cooperation that would be impossible in a one-shot game.</li>\n<li><strong>Information:</strong> Imperfect information within each round (simultaneous moves), but perfect information regarding the history of moves (each firm sees what the other produced in previous rounds).</li>\n<li><strong>Asymmetries:</strong> Generally modeled as symmetric (identical marginal costs and product quality), though asymmetries in production capacity or costs can exist.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Define Strategy Spaces</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Available Strategies:</strong> In each period, a firm chooses a quantity $q_i \\in [0, \\infty)$.</li>\n<li><strong>Discrete vs. Continuous:</strong> While quantity is technically continuous, for strategic analysis, we often categorize choices into:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Collude ($q_C$):</strong> Producing half of the monopoly output.</li>\n<li><strong>Cournot-Nash ($q_N$):</strong> The standard equilibrium where neither firm can improve profit by changing quantity alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Defect/Cheat ($q_D$):</strong> Producing the &quot;Best Response&quot; to the other firm's collusion to capture more market share.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Constraints:</strong> Production is limited by capacity ($q \\ge 0$) and the fact that total quantity ($Q$) eventually drives price below marginal cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Characterize Payoffs</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objectives:</strong> Maximization of the present value of the stream of profits over time.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependence:</strong> Payoffs are highly interdependent. If Firm A increases $q_A$, the market price $P$ drops, reducing Firm B’s profit.</li>\n<li><strong>Transferability:</strong> Non-transferable. Profits are earned individually based on market sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Key Features</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Timing:</strong> Simultaneous moves within each period.</li>\n<li><strong>Signaling:</strong> In a repeated game, a firm might produce a low quantity in period 1 to &quot;signal&quot; a desire to collude.</li>\n<li><strong>Discount Factor ($\\delta$):</strong> The value firms place on future profits. If $\\delta$ is high (firms are patient), collusion is more likely to be stable.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 2: Stage-Game Payoff Matrix</h3>\n<p>To construct a clear matrix, we simplify the continuous quantity choices into three discrete strategic stances.</p>\n<p><strong>Assumptions for Numerical Values:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Market Demand:</strong> $P = 100 - (q_A + q_B)$</li>\n<li><strong>Marginal Cost:</strong> $c = 10$</li>\n<li><strong>Monopoly Output (Collusion):</strong> Total $Q=45$; each firm produces <strong>22.5</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cournot-Nash Output:</strong> Each firm produces <strong>30</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Defection Output:</strong> If one colludes (22.5), the other's best response is <strong>33.75</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th align=\"left\">Firm A \\ Firm B</th><th align=\"left\">Collude ($q=22.5$)</th><th align=\"left\">Compete/Nash ($q=30$)</th><th align=\"left\">Defect/Cheat ($q=33.75$)</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Collude</strong></td><td align=\"left\">(1012, 1012)</td><td align=\"left\">(787, 1050)</td><td align=\"left\">(759, 1139)</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Compete/Nash</strong></td><td align=\"left\">(1050, 787)</td><td align=\"left\">(900, 900)</td><td align=\"left\">(843, 949)</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Defect/Cheat</strong></td><td align=\"left\">(1139, 759)</td><td align=\"left\">(949, 843)</td><td align=\"left\">(885, 885)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 3: Outcome Explanations</h3>\n<h4>1. Collude / Collude (The Cooperative Outcome)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Payoff:</strong> (1012, 1012)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> Both firms restrict output to act like a single monopolist. This results in the highest <strong>joint</strong> profit. However, this is unstable in a one-shot game because both firms have an incentive to &quot;cheat&quot; and produce more to capture more profit.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Compete / Compete (The Cournot-Nash Equilibrium)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Payoff:</strong> (900, 900)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> This is the standard non-cooperative equilibrium. Neither firm can increase its profit by changing its quantity given the other's choice. It is lower than the collusion payoff but higher than the &quot;Defect/Defect&quot; outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Defect / Collude (The &quot;Sucker&quot; Outcome)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Payoff:</strong> (1139, 759)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> Firm A &quot;cheats&quot; on the agreement by producing more ($q=33.75$) while Firm B remains faithful to the low-quantity agreement ($q=22.5$). Firm A captures a massive market share at a relatively high price, while Firm B’s profit is cannibalized.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Defect / Defect (The Overproduction Trap)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Payoff:</strong> (885, 885)</li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> Both firms attempt to cheat the other simultaneously. The market becomes flooded with goods, causing the price to crash. Both firms end up worse off than if they had simply stayed at the Cournot-Nash level.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Strategic Analysis of the Repeated Game</h3>\n<p>In the <strong>repeated</strong> version of this game, the firms look at the matrix differently:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The Temptation:</strong> In any single round, the move from <em>Collude</em> to <em>Defect</em> gains a firm +127 (1139 - 1012).</li>\n<li><strong>The Punishment:</strong> If Firm B uses a &quot;Grim Trigger&quot; strategy, they will move to <em>Compete/Nash</em> forever if Firm A defects. This costs Firm A -112 (1012 - 900) every single period for the rest of time.</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> If the discount factor ($\\delta$) is high enough (specifically $\\delta \\ge 0.53$ in this model), the long-term loss of 112 per period outweighs the one-time gain of 127, and <strong>stable collusion becomes the rational equilibrium.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" completion-message response-message\"></div></div>"